# Internships (playtest 2, X12; with M16): a shared backbone every placement draws (`plan: internship`; its
# performance through `perform internship`, whichever host you are with), and for each host one card that changes
# something beyond the summer. The everyday cards per host are in pools/plans_thesis.grim.

# ---------------- The backbone ----------------

@event intern_midsummer_review
title: The Midsummer Review
plan: internship
class: milestone
tone: tense
repeat: 4
when: month == 12
weight: 14
art: scroll
tags: study job
scene: A supervisor's desk with a single sheet of notes face down, a student sitting opposite with hands folded too tightly.
text: {Halfway through the placement, your supervisor asks to see you. There's a single sheet of notes on the desk, face down.|The midsummer review again: the desk, the sheet, the tea nobody drinks.|You know the review's shape by now. You still don't know what's on the sheet.} {performance(internship) >= 60: Your supervisor is almost smiling.|{performance(internship) < 40: Your supervisor is not smiling.|Your supervisor's face gives nothing away.}}
> left: Ask for honest criticism
  perform internship +small
  mood -small
  say: {You get it, three things you do badly, in order. You fix two of them by the end of the summer. Your supervisor notices both.|"Most interns ask for praise," your supervisor says, and turns the sheet over. It's a long list. You're grateful for every line, eventually.}
> right: Ask what you've done well
  mood +small
  perform internship -tiny
  say: {Your supervisor reads the good half aloud. You leave glowing, and never hear the other half.|"Your handwriting," your supervisor says, after a pause, "is excellent." You decide to take it.}

@event intern_credit_taken
title: Someone Else's Name
plan: internship
class: incident
tone: tense
repeat: 3
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: study job
scene: A report pinned on a wall, a student staring at the name at the bottom of it, which is not their own.
text: {The report you spent a week on is pinned up in the corridor, praised by everyone, with another intern's name at the bottom.|Another piece of your work, another name on it.|It's happened again, and this time the other intern doesn't even look away.} {Your supervisor is reading it with approval.|The other intern is being congratulated.|Your notes are still on your desk, in your hand.}
> left: Take it to your supervisor
  challenge negotiate journeyman+:
    win:
      perform internship +medium
      mood -tiny
      say: {You bring your notes, your drafts, your dates. Your supervisor reads them in silence and changes the name in red ink. The corridor goes very quiet around you.|You make your case calmly. The name is changed. The other intern doesn't speak to you again, which is fine.}
    lose:
      perform internship -small
      mood -small
      say: {Your supervisor hears you out and says it's "a matter for the two of you". It isn't. You go back to your desk with your face burning.|Without proof enough, you sound petty, and you know it. The name stays.}
> right: Let it go and work faster
  perform internship +tiny
  mood -medium
  say: {You say nothing and do the next one twice as well, in ink that's hard to copy. It doesn't feel like justice. It feels like work.|You let it go. You write your name on every page from then on, small, in the corner.}

@event intern_city_evening
title: An Evening Off
plan: internship
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: lantern
tags: social
scene: A lamplit street far from the academy, interns spilling out of a busy inn, one looking back at the lit window of the workplace.
text: {{member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): The other interns are going out tonight, three miles to a village dance, with a fiddler and a supper laid on at a few coins a head.|The other interns are going out tonight, to an inn with music and a stall that sells fried bread at midnight.}|Another evening off, and the others are already at the door.|"Coming?" says the intern with the biscuits. "Last time you said next time."} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The track is dark, but somebody has a lantern.|It's the only dance for thirty miles.}|{The streets are bright.|There's a fair on the river.|Somebody knows a band.}} Your work for tomorrow isn't done.
> left: Go out with them
  mood +medium
  perform internship -tiny
  gold -tiny
  say: {You pay your share of the supper, dance badly, laugh well and walk home at two in the morning, arm in arm with people you met last month. Tomorrow's work is a little worse for it.|You go, and stand your round. You learn more about the place from the other interns in one evening than from a month of briefings, and remember half of it.}
> right: Finish tomorrow's work
  perform internship +small
  mood -small
  say: {You finish the work by lamplight. You hear them come home, singing. In the morning your work is the best on the table.|You stay in. The work is good. The evening is long.}

@event intern_letter_from_campus
title: A Letter From the Castle
plan: internship
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: letter
tags: social
cast:
  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 8 and tagged(friend, student)
scene: A letter open on a desk far from the academy, its margins full of little drawings of the castle, a student smiling at it.
text: {A letter from {friend}, forwarded twice, with the castle drawn in the margins.|Another letter from campus, this one from {friend}.|{friend}'s handwriting on the envelope. You open it before your coat is off.} {The letter misses you, in a roundabout way.|{They} {are} bored without you, {they} say{s}.|There's gossip, and a pressed flower.} Answering it properly would take the evening.
> left: Write back at length
  rel friend +small
  perform internship -tiny
  say: {Six pages, with drawings of your own. You post it at dawn and are late for the briefing, and don't mind.|You write back everything: the work, the place, the interns. It's like talking to {them}.}
> right: Send a quick note
  perform internship +tiny
  rel friend -tiny
  say: {"Busy. Miss you. More soon." It's true, and it's thin, and you know it.|Three lines and a promise. You mean to write more. The summer is very full.}

# ---------------- One card per host that reaches past the summer ----------------

@event intern_conclave_your_ward
title: A Ward of Your Own
track: intern_conclave
class: milestone
tone: tense
weight: 18
art: shield
tags: study magic
scene: A circular test chamber, a student at the centre holding up a shimmering ward against a bolt of light, researchers behind thick glass on their feet.
text: The Conclave is testing a new ward, and the researcher who designed it is ill. "You've read every note," says your supervisor. "Cast it. If it holds, it's yours to keep, and your name goes in the paper." Behind the glass, half the Conclave has come to watch.
> left: Cast it
  wage
  challenge spell expert:
    win:
      perform intern_conclave +large
      learn_spell ward
      renown +small
      say: The bolt hits the ward and stops, humming, an inch from your nose. Behind the glass, someone starts clapping. Your name goes in the paper, third of four, and the ward is yours for good.
    lose:
      perform intern_conclave -small
      health -medium
      say: The ward holds for half a heartbeat and then doesn't. You wake on the floor with your hair standing up and a researcher fanning you with your own notes.
> right: Ask them to wait for the designer
  wage
  perform intern_conclave -tiny
  lore +small
  say: "Sensible," says your supervisor, in a voice that means "disappointing". The designer recovers, casts it, and thanks you in the paper's footnotes for the notes you kept.

@event intern_alchemists_safety
title: The Cracked Vat
track: intern_alchemists
class: milestone
tone: grim
weight: 18
art: cauldron
tags: job danger
scene: A huge iron vat in an alchemical works, a hairline crack weeping green steam, workers walking past it without looking.
text: The seventh vat at the Guild works has a crack in it. Everyone knows. The foreman says it'll last the season, and the season's quota depends on it. Workers walk past it without looking. You've seen what the steam does to brass.
> left: Report it to the Guild masters
  wage
  alignment +small
  perform intern_alchemists -small
  mood -small
  say: The masters close the seventh vat for a week and the foreman doesn't speak to you again. In the third week the replacement vat arrives, and an old worker shakes your hand without a word.
> right: Mend it yourself, quietly
  wage
  challenge brew expert:
    win:
      perform intern_alchemists +medium
      alchemy +medium
      say: You seal it from the inside in the night, with a sealant of your own. It holds through the season. The foreman never knows. The old worker does, somehow, and leaves an apple on your bench every morning.
    lose:
      health -large
      perform intern_alchemists -small
      say: The steam gets you as the seal gives. You wake in the works infirmary with blistered hands, and the vat is closed anyway.

@event intern_expedition_new_star
title: A Star Nobody Charted
track: intern_expedition
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 18
art: star
tags: study magic
scene: A telescope on a high plateau at night, a student with an eye to the eyepiece and a chart on their knee, a single new star circled in red.
text: The lead gives you tonight's watch at the eyepiece, alone. At three in the morning there's a star in it that isn't on any chart: small, steady, blue. The expedition lead is asleep. By custom, whoever records a new star first may name it.
> left: Wake the lead and share it
  wage
  perform intern_expedition +medium
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  say: The lead comes in a nightshirt and stares until dawn. The star goes in the record under the expedition's name and yours, and the whole camp toasts it at breakfast.
> right: Record it in your own name
  wage
  renown +medium
  perform intern_expedition +small
  mood -tiny
  say: You write it into the log: the time, the place, a name you choose. It's yours for as long as there are charts. At breakfast the lead reads the log twice, and is polite to you all week, which is worse.

@event intern_field_station_foal
title: The Orphaned Foal
track: intern_field_station
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 18
art: paw
tags: nature
scene: A newborn hippogriff foal shivering in the heather on the moor, a student kneeling with a blanket, the field station lamps far off.
text: On the moor, a hippogriff foal, newborn and alone. The station's rule is plain: you count wild creatures, you don't raise them. The station chief says it won't last the night. It looks at you as if you were the only warm thing on the moor, which you are.
> left: Take it back and raise it
  wage
  vigor +small
  health -small
  alignment +small
  perform intern_field_station -small
  rel brask +small
  say: You feed it every two hours for a month and break the rule every one of them. By summer's end it flies. Back at Lumenhold, Professor Brask hears the story and invites you to supper. "Rules," she says, "are for people who've never held a foal."
> right: Count it and leave it
  wage
  perform intern_field_station +small
  mood -medium
  say: You write it down: one foal, alone, the date. You don't sleep. In the morning the chief tells you its mother came back in the night. You never find out if that's true.

@event intern_thornwick_offer
title: An Offer From Thornwick
track: intern_thornwick
class: milestone
tone: tense
weight: 18
art: letter
when: month == 12
tags: study
scene: A lacquered office at a rival academy, a smiling dean sliding a sealed offer across a desk, a window full of unfamiliar towers.
text: Thornwick's dean asks to see you on your last week. "Transfer to us," she says, sliding a sealed offer across the desk. "Full scholarship, your own lab bench, a seat in our illusion faculty in time. Lumenhold never valued glamour. We do." She's sincere, and that's the danger.
> left: Decline, warmly
  wage
  perform intern_thornwick +small
  rel vey +small
  say: "Lumenhold is home," you say. She smiles as if she expected it. "The offer stands." Back at the castle, Professor Vey hears about it somehow and gives you an approving look that lasts all term.
> right: Keep the offer, unopened
  wage
  gold +medium
  renown +small
  flag thornwick_offer
  mood -small
  say: You keep it, sealed, in the bottom of your trunk. Thornwick sends a stipend "as a courtesy". Knowing there's a door out makes some evenings easier, and some harder.

@event intern_unseen_office_file
title: The File With Your Name
track: intern_unseen_office
class: milestone
tone: eerie
weight: 18
art: eye
tags: mystery
scene: A windowless office lined with drawers, a student holding open a thin file with their own name on the label, a clerk's empty chair behind them.
text: Sorting files for the Office, you find one with your name on it. It's thin. The clerk has gone to lunch. The last page is about Lumenhold, and the lake, and a tower nobody has opened in a century.
> left: Read it all
  wage
  guile +small
  find lead_seventh_tower
  perform intern_unseen_office -small
  say: They know where you sleep and what you eat, which is unsettling. The last page is mostly blacked out. What's left says the seventh tower "is to remain closed", and nothing about why. When the clerk comes back you are filing something else. The clerk looks at you a little too long.
> right: Put it back unread
  wage
  perform intern_unseen_office +medium
  mood -small
  say: You put it back. The clerk returns, checks the drawer and smiles for the first time all summer. "The Office notices who doesn't look," the clerk says. You didn't look. You'll wonder for years.
